North Korea is warning the US could face  what it calls an unimaginable strike at  any time a new statement from Pyongyang  criticizes ongoing joint military drills  between the u.s. and South Korea it says  the two countries are war mongers and  the root cause of a nuclear war New York  Times opinion page communist Nicholas  Kristof spent five days inside North  Korea and wrote a series of articles  about his experiences he discovered  North Korea is galvanizing its people to  expect a nuclear war with the u.s.  Nikolas joins us here at the table good  morning good morning this is your third  trip to North Korea right that's correct  and so how was this different from what  you've seen in the past so it was very  different  I mean North Korea is always a bizarre  place and there's always a certain  amount of over-the-top rhetoric but this  time the country has really galvanized  for war there's constant talk about  missile attacks on the US there are  billboards around the country showing  missiles destroying the US Capitol  destroying the US flag there is  discussion about how a nuclear war with  the u.s. is inevitable and maybe most  striking about how North Korea will  defeat the u.s. in this nuclear war has  not only survivable but but actually  winnable they're convinced they they  would win if a war took place  astonishingly they are convinced that  that they would win and that the u.s.  would then retreat ignominiously from  the korean peninsula from japan from  asia and kim jongmin would unite the  korean peninsula are they so confident  Nick well I mean of course it's always  very difficult to distinguish what they  say versus what they fail on but they  have a huge network of underground  tunnels and underground fortifications  and their narrative is also that they  have repeatedly defeated the US and the  Korean War in a series of incidents  across the the DMZ and so and there's  always a danger of course the dictators  come to believe their own propaganda and  I'm nervous if that is what is happening  right now were you nervous and afraid  while you were there yourself but you  knew you had handlers the whole time it  didn't seem like you were able to move  freely as you have been in the past  that's right I mean look reporting in  North Korea you were always very  constrained  but in the past I was able to stay in  the center of the city in a hotel to  walk around this time the North Korean  Foreign Ministry had handlers that just  clung to me like a limpet they kept me  at a guarded foreign ministry compound  in the rice paddies outside of town and  I thought oh this is to protect the  North Koreans from my vigorous  questioning but no it turns out that  they were there to protect me from the  security services who might not be on  board with having an American journalist  in town and that was so that was kidnap  you and take you yeah you know I don't  think it was so much that the security  forces would suddenly grab me I think it  was more of a concern that I might  engage in some kind of mischief and then  the security forces would grab me and  then there would be a problem we know  North Korea is probably the biggest  national security test that that Trump  will face when I was there in May and  interviewed president moon the new  president of South Korea he talked about  opening up a bilateral dialogue Trump  talked about that on the campaign he  said talking sitting knee to knee with  the North Korean leader Kim Jong on any  sense that that dialogue can or will  happen one of the sad thing is is that  the the doves if you will the people  trying to find an exit ramp in North  Korea just like those in Washington seem  to be marginalized and so the hardliners  seemed to be ascendant both in the North  Korean capital Pyongyang and in  Washington so I was struck that the  North Koreans were not very interested  in in talks so that's interesting  because you know President Obama's  policy was one of strategic patience  right there wasn't this bellicose  rhetoric some people criticize that as  in letting the North Koreans build up  this weaponry but now you have this very  tough talk from the president's mouth  and on Twitter how are the North Koreans  using that so I think that the president  Trump's talk has been counterproductive  it was striking that the North Koreans  are leveraging it for their own  propaganda  so your average North Korea knows  nothing about otto warmbier for example  though that doesn't penetrate they know  all about president Trump's threat to  totally destroy North Korea because it  fits into their narrative but this is  the other part of it we did a story with  David Newton Felder who was in North  dozens of times and he talked about all  of North Korea's not kim jeong-hoon I  mean there's we're talking about  millions of people there too who don't  necessarily don't know about all of this  and and wouldn't necessarily agree with  what their leader is doing you know it's  very hard to know what ordinary North  Koreans think but and in North Korea you  don't get a very good sense of that but  I must say talking to defectors you get  the sense that the mind control  controlling every bit of information  having a speaker in people's homes to to  bring in propaganda not having radio  dials that that works pretty well to get  people to essentially believe in the  regime except closer to the Chinese  board or closer to the Chinese border  they do hear alternative truths and they  begin to question things as I try to  live their lives avoiding nuclear war  and we're glad you're back safely so am  I so is my wife yes thank you
 

